{
  "id": "ep232",
  "duration": 56.658,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.452,
      "text": "A billion-year-old star is glowing with elements that decay in decades.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of WD 0816-310, a magnetic white dwarf with a metal scar (eso2403a).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_WD_0816-310,_a_magnetic_white_dwarf_with_a_metal_scar_(eso2403a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.767,
          "end": 4.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.452,
      "end": 10.847,
      "text": "Our own Sun is mostly hydrogen, with heavier elements making up less than one percent of its mass.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Image of Sun From NASA\u2019s Solar Dynamics Observatory (PIA26681).tiff",
        "credit": "NASA/GSFC/Solar Dynamics Observatory",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image_of_Sun_From_NASA%E2%80%99s_Solar_Dynamics_Observatory_(PIA26681).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.467,
          "end": 8.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.533,
          "end": 10.833,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.847,
      "end": 20.492,
      "text": "Located 356 light-years away in the southern constellation Centaurus, Przybylski's Star completely flips that familiar recipe.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Eso2118d.jpg",
        "credit": "ESO, IAU and Sky & Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eso2118d.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.833,
          "end": 16.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 16.1,
          "end": 20.5,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.492,
      "end": 30.587,
      "text": "In 1961, astronomer Antoni Przybylski found rare-earth metals like holmium enriched by over 1,000 times solar levels.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Antoni Przybylski SLNSW FL16054294.jpg",
        "credit": "unknown",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoni_Przybylski_SLNSW_FL16054294.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.5,
          "end": 25.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 25.533,
          "end": 30.6,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.587,
      "end": 39.432,
      "text": "Later studies reported even stranger things: short-lived radioactive elements like promethium, which decays in under 18 years.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Tableau p\u00e9riodique des \u00e9l\u00e9ments simplifi\u00e9.png",
        "credit": "Scaler,Michka B",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tableau_p%C3%A9riodique_des_%C3%A9l%C3%A9ments_simplifi%C3%A9.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.6,
          "end": 35.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 35.833,
          "end": 39.433,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.432,
      "end": 45.582,
      "text": "That sparked wild theories of alien civilizations dumping radioactive waste inside.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Dyson sphere.png",
        "credit": "LoveEmployee",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dyson_sphere.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.433,
          "end": 43.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 43.033,
          "end": 45.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 45.582,
      "end": 50.752,
      "text": "But overlapping light patterns likely explain away the radioactive elements.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Blue spectrum of HD126587 (noao0003a).tiff",
        "credit": "NOIRLab/NSF/AURA",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_spectrum_of_HD126587_(noao0003a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 45.567,
          "end": 50.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.752,
      "end": 56.308,
      "text": "Researchers are still investigating the seemingly impossible chemical makeup of this.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of WD 0816-310, a magnetic white dwarf with a metal scar (eso2403a).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
